    "body": "## What\n\nA **trochoidal toolpath strategy**: circular loops whose centre advances along the cut. It's the usual constant-engagement way to clear slots, narrow channels and corners, where a spiral or offset adaptive path would be forced into full-width (or a sudden jump in) radial engagement. Ship it as a selectable option for Adaptive clearing (2D and 3D levels), and as a standalone \"Trochoidal slot\" operation along a chain or centre line.\n\nThis is a feature request. The existing trochoid work (#5, #47, the M11.4 fallbacks) only covers Adaptive's *internal* fallback in slot-width channels and thin bands. This issue is the general, user-facing strategy.\n\n## Background (public literature)\n\n- **Geometry**, for a simple circular loop: x(φ) = B·cos φ, y(φ) = B·sin φ + S·φ/(2π), with B the loop radius and S the forward advance per revolution. For a slot of width W cut by a tool of diameter D, B = (W − D)/2. Epicycloidal and double-trochoid variants also exist.\n- **Engagement angle** θ = θ_out − θ_in: the centre angle of the cutter's contact arc with the *current* stock, read at each point along the path.\n- **Chip thickness** in the peripheral approximation is h(α) = f_z·sin α. On a trochoid the engagement window moves along the loop, so h varies; it isn't constant.\n- **How constant engagement is kept:** evaluate θ against the live stock, and when it exceeds the limit, shrink the loop, cut the forward step, or reshape the loop, and/or drop the feed where θ rises.\n- **References:**\n  - Otkur & Lazoglu, \"Trochoidal milling\", IJMTM 2007: chip-thickness and force model, double trochoid.\n  - Rauch, Duc & Hascoet, \"Improving trochoidal tool paths generation and implementation using process constraints modelling\", IJMTM 2009.\n  - B-spline trochoid optimisation with a maximum-engagement constraint, Int J Adv Manuf Technol 2022 (doi 10.1007/s00170-022-09527-z).\n  - Circular, trochoidal and epicycloidal parameterisations, Prod. Eng. 2021 (doi 10.1007/s11740-021-01060-4).\n\n## Proposed parameters (user parameters with defaults)\n\n- loop width (≤ the channel width − D, auto from the channel when unset)\n- forward step per loop\n- maximum engagement angle (or the equivalent optimal load)\n- return arc: cutting / non-cutting with microlift (height) / retract\n- feed on the cutting arc vs the return arc (the return arc at a higher \"air\" feed)\n- direction: climb / conventional\n- smoothing radius for the loop-to-loop transitions\n\n## Done means\n\n1. A trochoid generator in `crates/motion` that is engagement-controlled against the in-process stock: the loop and step adapt so θ ≤ the limit everywhere, and a refusal by name where the channel is narrower than the tool or the limit can't be met.\n2. Adaptive gets a \"trochoidal in slots/corners\" mode that switches from the spiral to trochoidal loops where the spiral would exceed the limit (merges with, or replaces, #5's internal fallback).\n3. A standalone Trochoidal slot operation along a picked chain or centre line, through `CreateOperation` / `EditOperationParams` with undo.\n4. The verifier judges the loops: no gouge, the engagement limit holds (a measured θ trace in tests), and microlift returns land on air (#47).\n5. Pins:\n   - a full-width slot with θ_max under the limit, compared with a straight slot at 180°;\n   - a corner that a spiral path overloads, cut within the limit;\n   - a too-narrow channel refused by name;\n   - post goldens on Fanuc, where arcs are written as G2/G3 when the post supports them.\n\nRelated: #5, #7, #44, #47 (Adaptive trochoid internals).\n",
